WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:04.790
Crowd: Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.910
John: Ohh, Hank, it's been an incredibly tense
and overwhelming week in my life, with lots

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:13.059
and lots and lots and lots and lots of interviews,
which I am not good at, but fortunately, I've

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:16.059
usually been with Nat Wolff, who makes everything
in my life better.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:19.631
And also I got to meet many thousands of Nerdfighters
around the country, more people wanted to

00:00:19.631 --> 00:00:21.890
say hello to you, by the way, hold on.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:24.710
Crowd: Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:28.000
Other crowd: Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.310
John: Also, I got my photograph taken a lot.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:34.520
There's this whole commodification of the
self involved in this marketing business that

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.800
I don't really understand and haven't processed
yet, but hopefully I'll make a video about

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:38.800
it someday.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.521
In the meantime, all I know is that the fancy
New York premiere of Paper Towns is tonight.

00:00:41.521 --> 00:00:44.790
By the way, thanks to everybody on Twitter
and Snapchat who helped me pick out my fancy

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:45.790
premiere suit.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:47.810
I may need your help later in choosing a tie,
by the way.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:52.250
The movie comes out on Friday, July 24th,
but on Thursday, which is the day after freaking

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.300
tomorrow, there's a special screening at a
bunch of theaters around the US and Canada.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.420
I know some of them are sold out, but some
of them aren't, so hopefully you can still

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.090
go to nightonthetowns.com and get tickets,
and then in addition to seeing the movie before

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.750
everyone else, you will also see a livestream
with the entire cast and musical performances,

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.440
it's going to be really fun.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.940
Also that livestream is gonna be hosted by
our friend, Tyler Oakley, who I've known for

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:15.000
like, eight years and I'm just so proud of
him and so excited that we get to celebrate

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.000
this together.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.860
Waaauugh, Hank, you might be able to tell
that I'm a little bit wound up right now.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.710
One of the ways I'm coping with my stress
is by reading reviews of Paper Towns, because

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:24.420
I just really really want people to see it
and find out what they think of it, and I've

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.160
just realized that when I earlier said that
people on Thursday would be the first people

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.560
to see the movie, I was, in fact, lying, because
the movie is already out in Brazil and Australia

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.970
and a couple of other places, and like, lots
of critics have seen it, and fortunately,

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.760
the reviews have been great, like, Variety
said that it was better than The Fault in

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:36.760
Our Stars.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.320
Speaking of which, Hank, when I was on tour,
exactly zero interviewers said the title of

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.380
The Fault in Our Stars correctly.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.110
They would be like, "Do you feel a lot of
pressure after all the success of No Fault

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:46.320
Under These Stars?"

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.439
And I would be like, "Well, I felt more pressure
before I realized that absolutely no one remembers

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:51.710
the title of that movie."

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:55.700
Right, but anyway, I've been obsessively reading
reviews, including reviews from Brazil, but

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.900
unfortunately, I do not speak Portuguese,
so I have been reading them via Google Translate

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.299
and they are very positive, I--I think.

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:06.000
"Distinguished representative of the school
John Hughes movie, Paper Cities has everything

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.920
to be the teen movie of the year.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.590
The result is a fresh and full length humor,
far from the melodrama of blame it star."

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.099
Solid work, Google Translate.

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:15.140
Blame it, star!

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:16.829
Anyway, comparisons to John Hughes are always
welcome.

00:02:16.829 --> 00:02:18.790
Here's another one, "It is a beautiful film.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:21.900
The Schreier--" I believe that's director
Jake Schreier, although it could be from The

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.120
Hobbit, "--works with actors Wolff and Face--"
Not sure who Face is, maybe Cara?

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.120
Maybe Justice? "--is magnificent.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.439
In the reunion of the two, the dialogue is
as accurate and interpretive and sincere Paper

00:02:32.439 --> 00:02:34.969
Cities that borders on perfection."

00:02:34.969 --> 00:02:37.780
And here's another one, Hank, "Moving away
from the book, literally the film crew approach

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.139
the writer in its essence.

00:02:39.139 --> 00:02:44.519
It's so exploited rites of passage to adulthood
without the mischievousness of John Hughes,

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:46.909
but with the melancholy of Walt Whitman."

00:02:46.909 --> 00:02:50.359
So yeah, no comparison to John Hughes there,
but instead Whitman.

00:02:50.359 --> 00:02:51.359
Also welcome.

00:02:51.359 --> 00:02:54.549
Somewhere in the grass underneath our boot
soles, Walt Whitman is like, "Yeah, I mean,

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:57.370
I thought the movie was okay, but let's not
get ahead of ourselves."

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:00.120
Anyway, Hank, I hope that you like the movie,
and that Nerdfighteria likes it, and thanks

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.610
to everyone who has shared this journey with
me.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.620
One last thing, something I've learned about
movie studios in the last two years is that

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.409
they're obsessed with hashtags.

00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:10.629
They're obsessed with them because it allows
them to, like, track the success of a campaign,

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:14.209
and I'm sure there will be lots of super official
hashtags around the Paper Towns movie, but

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:17.480
I mostly care about the responses from Nerdfighteria
and people who really care about the story,

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.959
so I thought that I would invent a fake hashtag,
and then I can look at that hashtag instead

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:21.980
of like, the official ones.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:23.909
Don't tell the studio, but there's a link
in the doobly-doo.

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:25.580
Nerdfighters, I hope that you like the movie.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:26.959
Hank, I will see you on Friday.

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:27.959
p.s.

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:30.879
Hank, I'm so sorry about your VidCon stress,
I hope you might find some comfort in these

00:03:30.879 --> 00:03:36.269
images of Pluto, a cold distant former planet
that humans have come together to better understand.

